\title{V1: a worker in steady state allocates nothing}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{gc}
\tag{store}
\tag{reference-engine}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/V1.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Agent "V1" (memory behaviour of the machine engine), 2026-11-24, 18:37Z onwards. Question (P7-F1): the collections are the 5 - 8 \% between one process with 8 workers and the hardware ceiling; who makes the garbage, and can a worker in steady state allocate (almost) nothing?}

\p{Short answer. The machine's part of the garbage is not "arrays regrown after each reset" but \strong{the 1 - 2 \% of the sessions whose one declaration needs more than the kept slab sizes} (8 MiB of records, 4 MiB of run words): each of them doubles its slabs copy by copy and drops them at its reset. On mathlib that is 4.9 GB of slabs and 0.56 GB of tables per pass = 39 \% of the 13.8 GB the 8 workers allocate. The prototype gives these arrays a \strong{process-wide pool} (taken at the growth, given back at the session's reset, softly held, expiring after 8,192 resets), hands the arrays of a closed session to the next session of the process, and counts every allocation. Measured (section 5): on cedar the machine then allocates nothing from the second pass on (1,847 MB -> 382 MB over three passes, 374 MB of them in the first), and a warm pipelined pass with 8 workers takes 2.39 s instead of 2.52 s on cedar, 1.81 instead of 1.88 s on Std, \strong{29.9 instead of 31.4 s on mathlib (-4.8 \%)} for the same peak RSS. On mathlib the machine's garbage only falls from 5.4 to 3.4 GB per pass at \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~}, because a heap that is 2.8 GB live takes softly held arrays back at every complete collection; the pause share stays near 10 \%, so \strong{the two targets of the brief (collections under 2 \%, within 2 \% of the ceiling) are not reached}. The other 61 \% of the workers' bytes is the \strong{reference kernel re-checking the units the machine declines} (\code{\verb~|rlvm.ref.IntIntMap~} 21 \%, the store overlay's index 17 \%, the rewriter memo 11 \%, the overlay's node array 9 \%): three small patches are proposed to their owners and were measured in private copies; their first version ran the image out of the 4 GB heap, the patches are the bounded version. The region question (section 6): the session already is two bump regions that a reset rewinds; a reset costs 8 - 9 us (0.2 - 0.3 \% of the worker CPU; the growths of the tables inside a session cost twenty times more); what a two-region design could buy is reuse (ticks), not memory.}

\p{Everything here is a prototype in private copies (\code{\verb~|work/v1/~}); nothing was published.}

\subtree{
\title{1. Method}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Trees.} \code{\verb~|work/v1/base/~} = \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~} as copied under the sandbox lock at 18:38:20Z (jar 8a8ea5644b24, the jar INT6 installed at 18:2xZ); \code{\verb~|work/v1/tree5/~} = base + the prototype (\code{\verb~|work/v1/tools/apply_machine.py~}, \code{\verb~|apply_machine2.py~} .. \code{\verb~|apply_machine5.py~} in this order: exact-string edits, so the patch is reproducible; the intermediate trees \code{\verb~|tree~} .. \code{\verb~|tree4~} are the versions measured on the way); \code{\verb~|work/v1/xbase/~}, \code{\verb~|xtree/~} = private copies of \code{\verb~|rlvm-store~} and \code{\verb~|rlvm-ref~} for the proposals (\code{\verb~|apply_x.py~}).}
\li{\strong{Distributions} (\code{\verb~|work/v1/tools/relink.sh~}, after P7's): the jars published at 18:27Z (driver 31fa08d9ce61, store f85c730603b8, ref ca1f9bd918bb, load 26242def2701, admit 398a117d4ad8) with the machine jar replaced; native image \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~} as P7's. \code{\verb~|dist-base-ni~} (machine 8a8ea5644b24), \code{\verb~|dist-v1~} (first prototype: per-worker retention), \code{\verb~|dist-v2~} (shared pool, 2 arrays per size, machine 68e9bdf1aeef), \code{\verb~|dist-v3~} (pool bounded by bytes, 1d6a18219223), \code{\verb~|dist-v4~} (the delivered tree, 4e129e8cb0c7); in each prototype image \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.big=off -Drlvm.machine.spares=0~} gives the base behaviour; \code{\verb~|dist-x2~}, \code{\verb~|dist-x3~} (= v2 / v3 + the store and reference proposals, first and bounded version).}
\li{\strong{Allocation account.} JVM launcher, JFR with \code{\verb~|jdk.ObjectAllocationOutsideTLAB~} and \code{\verb~|jdk.ObjectAllocationInNewTLAB~} (stack traces), TLABs fixed at 32 KiB (\code{\verb~|-XX:TLABSize=32k -XX:-ResizeTLAB~}): every array of 32 KiB or more is counted exactly, the rest is sampled every 32 KiB; exact totals per thread from \code{\verb~|jdk.ThreadAllocationStatistics~} (\code{\verb~|work/v1/tools/jfr.sh~}, \code{\verb~|JfrAlloc.java~}; results \code{\verb~|work/v1/data/jfr/*.alloc.txt~}). The machine's own census (new statistics \code{\verb~|machine.alloc.grow|reset|pooled.<array>.2^k~}, \code{\verb~|machine.session.records.2^k~}, \code{\verb~|machine.resetNanos~}, \code{\verb~|machine.growNanos~}; \code{\verb~|work/v1/tools/census.sh~}, \code{\verb~|census.py~}).}
\li{\strong{Timing.} Every wall-clock number: \src{bench/warm.sh} rows inside \src{bench/quiet.py} windows (\code{\verb~|work/v1/tools/w/*.sh~}, several rows per window, the variants interleaved), native image, machine engine with its default switches (\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, session cap 1 MiB), \code{\verb~|load=pipelined~}, \code{\verb~|passes-mode=reload~} (every pass loads and checks), \code{\verb~|-XX:+PrintGC~}; per pass: wall, collections, pause time, bytes freed by the collections of the pass (\code{\verb~|work/v1/tools/passgc.py~} on the raw logs in \code{\verb~|work/v1/data/warm-logs/~}; rows in \code{\verb~|work/v1/data/warm.tsv~}). mathlib: \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~}. Single windows; differences below 2 - 3 \% are not results.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The account: who allocates what}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 mathlib, one batch pass, 8 workers (JVM, 13 GB heap, announced; \code{\verb~|base-mathlib-t8.alloc.txt~})}

\p{17.4 GB in all: workers 13.83 GB, loader thread 3.11 GB, environment thread 0.42 GB (exact). The workers' bytes:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Site}\th{GB}\th{Share}\th{What it is}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Machine.copyOrNull~} < \code{\verb~|growRecords~} / \code{\verb~|growRuns~}}\td{4.89}\td{35 \%}\td{slab doublings ABOVE the kept sizes: record slab to 16 MiB x 71, 32 MiB x 27, 64 MiB x 4 (2.37 GB); run slab to 8 MiB x 123, 16 MiB x 35, 32 MiB x 11 (1.99 GB); below the kept sizes only the first growth of each worker (0.19 GB)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Machine.newTable~}}\td{0.56}\td{4 \%}\td{intern tables above the 4 MiB pool (8 MiB x 29, 16 MiB x 4), pair tables above it (8 MiB x 7, 16 MiB x 1), and each worker's first table of every size}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|rlvm.ref.IntIntMap.allocate~}}\td{2.89}\td{21 \%}\td{the reference kernel's caches: cut back to 256 entries at \code{\verb~|clear()~} when large or sparse, doubled again in the next large declaration (100,000 tables of 4 KiB - 16 MiB)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Overlay.rehash~} (store)}\td{2.37}\td{17 \%}\td{the hash-consing index of the overlay: cut back to 128 KiB by \code{\verb~|resetTo~} (4,354 new tables), doubled again by \code{\verb~|insert~} (3,567 new tables of 256 KiB - 64 MiB)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|TermOps.Memo.widen~} (store)}\td{1.54}\td{11 \%}\td{two temporaries per doubling of the rewriter's memo; every call starts at 64 slots}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|Overlay.grow~} + \code{\verb~|releaseNodes~} (store)}\td{1.40}\td{10 \%}\td{the overlay's node array: 0.9 GB is ONE declaration of the reference kernel that grows it to 256 MiB; the rest the first growth of every lane, and four shrinks back to 32 MiB}}
\tr{\td{level overlays (\code{\verb~|IntListStore~}, \code{\verb~|LevelStore.<init>~})}\td{0.24}\td{2 \%}\td{new lanes (3 per worker: machine, strict, reference)}}
\tr{\td{everything else of the machine}\td{< 0.01}\td{}\td{per-unit set-up, statistics, literals, \code{\verb~|Values.Ext~}, \code{\verb~|StrRed~}: nothing}}
}

\p{Where the stack is complete (JFR keeps 64 frames; the reference kernel recurses deeper), the \code{\verb~|IntIntMap~}, \code{\verb~|Overlay~} and \code{\verb~|Memo~} rows come from \code{\verb~|Worker.reference > StoreLane.admit > RefSession.check~}: the re-check of a unit the machine declined (6,711 units of mathlib, the eager-reduction rule; agent E1 works on keeping them on the machine, which would remove most of these four rows at the source).}

\p{\strong{Correction of P7-F1}: the machine's session arrays are not grown again after each reset. Since F25 (NOTES-M6) slabs are kept up to 8 MiB / 4 MiB and tables are pooled up to 4 MiB; a session at the 1 MiB cap needs 1 - 2 MiB of records. What is grown again is the tail of the session-size distribution (machine census, mathlib slice / cedar / Std: 2,300 - 2,500 of 3,200 - 5,200 sessions end with 16,000 - 32,000 records, 1 - 12 per corpus with more than 131,000): about 280 of the 14,000 sessions of a mathlib pass, 20 KB per unit on average but 17 MB per such session.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 cedar and Std, one cold pass, 8 workers (JVM; machine census with the base behaviour)}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{cedar}\th{Std}\th{mathlib slice (\code{\verb~|Mathlib.Data.Nat.Basic~}, 114,861 units)}}
\tr{\td{sessions (resets), of them by the machine's cap}\td{5,224 / 2,531}\td{3,477 / 1,987}\td{3,183 / 1,511}}
\tr{\td{bytes in use at a reset (mean)}\td{839 KB}\td{912 KB}\td{804 KB}}
\tr{\td{new machine arrays in the pass}\td{585 MB}\td{298 MB}\td{290 MB}}
\tr{\td{- of it above the kept sizes (regrown per large session)}\td{327 MB (17 + 7 run slabs, 3 record slabs)}\td{34 MB}\td{17 MB}}
\tr{\td{- of it the first growth of each of the 8 workers (once per session object)}\td{about 260 MB}\td{265 MB}\td{273 MB}}
\tr{\td{workers' bytes in all (JFR, exact)}\td{1,165 MB}\td{}\td{}}
}

\p{On the mid-size corpora the machine's garbage is mostly the \strong{first growth of each worker's arrays} (base sizes are small on purpose: 512 KiB of records, 128 KiB tables; each worker doubles its way to the kept sizes once: 33 - 37 MB of copies per worker). That is paid once per session object, and \code{\verb~|-X passes~} makes new sessions (new lanes, new engines) for every pass: in the owner's warm metric it is paid in every pass.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 What a reset and a growth cost in CPU}

\p{New statistics \code{\verb~|machine.resetNanos~} / \code{\verb~|machine.growNanos~} (wall time inside \code{\verb~|Machine.reset()~} and inside the growths of slabs and tables; only under statistics).}

\table{
\tr{\th{run (native image, 8 workers, pipelined, statistics on)}\th{sessions (resets)}\th{units per session}\th{in use at a reset}\th{inside resets}\th{per reset}\th{inside growths}\th{worker CPU of the pass}}
\tr{\td{cedar, quiet window, third pass (shared-2)}\td{4,987 (2,689 by the machine's cap)}\td{29}\td{872 KB}\td{0.045 s}\td{9.1 us}\td{0.81 s}\td{17.3 s}}
\tr{\td{Std, quiet window, third pass (shared-2)}\td{3,254 (2,124)}\td{31}\td{965 KB}\td{0.029 s}\td{8.9 us}\td{0.56 s}\td{12.7 s}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, quiet window, second pass (shared-2)}\td{59,534 (24,187)}\td{12}\td{725 KB}\td{0.50 s}\td{8.4 us}\td{12.2 s}\td{216 s}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, a host slot (loaded), one pass, base behaviour}\td{60,255 (23,691)}\td{12}\td{716 KB}\td{1.14 s}\td{18.9 us}\td{31.9 s}\td{}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, a host slot (loaded), one pass, delivered tree}\td{60,190 (23,719)}\td{12}\td{717 KB}\td{1.13 s}\td{18.8 us}\td{29.7 s}\td{}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A reset is cheap}: 8 - 9 us on a quiet core, 0.2 - 0.3 \% of the worker CPU (the tables are cleared in O(used); the slabs are not touched). P7's "14,000 resets per pass" was the 4 MiB cap; at the 1 MiB cap mathlib has 60,000 sessions per pass, one per 12 units.}
\li{\strong{The growths cost twenty times more}: 4.4 - 5.6 \% of the worker CPU is spent inside them (an upper bound: a collection that a growth's allocation starts is counted, 3.1 s of the 12.2 s on mathlib at most). Beyond the fresh arrays and the slab copies it is the re-probe of a session's records and runs when a table doubles, which happens in nearly every session: at the 1 MiB cap a session ends with 16,000 - 32,000 records and the base \code{\verb~|vtab~} has 2^15 slots (on cedar 2,666 of 5,058 sessions took the pooled 2^16 table, 794 the next \code{\verb~|rtab~}, 730 the next pair table). Finding F-V1-M3. A base \code{\verb~|vtab~} of 2^16 slots alone (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.sizes=14,16,16,14,12,10~}, one row each in the same window) did not show: cedar 2.40 s against 2.40 / 2.38 s, Std 1.78 s against 1.81 / 1.81 s (inside the spread). The tables to size are a sweep for M6 (vtab, rtab and pairs together, against the cache), not done here.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The prototype (owner M6: \code{\verb~|Machine.java~}, \code{\verb~|MachineSession.java~}, \code{\verb~|MachineEngine.java~})}

\p{Patch: \code{\verb~|work/v1/patches/rlvm-machine-M6/0001-large-arrays-shared-spares-census.patch~} (= \code{\verb~|work/v1/tree5~}, jar 4e129e8cb0c7; against the main tree as of 18:38Z; the three files were unchanged in the main tree at 20:40Z). Parts, separable:}

\ol{
\li{\p{\strong{Large arrays are shared} (\code{\verb~|Machine.Big~}, \code{\verb~|Sizes.bigShared~}, \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.big=shared|own|off~}, default \code{\verb~|shared~}). A slab that grows past its kept size takes its next size from a process-wide pool (only the words in use are copied; a miss allocates as before) and the array goes back to the pool at the session's reset; the same for intern and pair tables above 2^20 slots (pooled all-zero). The session's own kept arrays and table pools are as before, so the reset path of the 98 \% of the sessions is unchanged. Rules that make it safe:}
\ul{
\li{an array that a growth replaced, or that the session is done with, is handed to the pool at the END of \code{\verb~|reset()~}, never while a judgment runs: code that still holds a replaced array (a local copy of \code{\verb~|m.vh~}) reads what it read before, exactly as when the old array was garbage (a first version gave it away at the growth). Until then it waits softly held, and an array above the pooled size is not kept at all, so a giant declaration needs no more memory than before (a version that held the replaced steps strongly - 16 + 32 + .. + 256 MiB under a 512 MiB slab - ran M8's gate out of its heap on \code{\verb~|good/perf/magma-list-deep-n36~});}
\li{not under a debug mode (\code{\verb~|poison~} wants zeroed slabs);}
\li{the pool holds its arrays through \code{\verb~|SoftReference~}s, and \code{\verb~|Machine.heapAllows~} empties it before it refuses a growth: pooled memory is never the reason for an \code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} or for an \code{\verb~|E_MEM~} decline;}
\li{the two pools hold at most a sixteenth of the maximum heap together, at most 512 MiB (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.big.mib~}), no array above 64 MiB (\code{\verb~|big.maxLog2~}), and an array that no session took for 8,192 resets (all machines; \code{\verb~|big.decay~}) goes back to the heap: one huge declaration does not pin memory for ever, and what is held is a property of the process, not 8 workers times the largest declaration;}
\li{a slab may take a pooled array of up to four times the size it asked for (a larger slab costs nothing and saves the next doubling): the large sessions come in clusters, and the sizes on hand are rarely the ones asked for.}
}}
\li{\strong{Spare session arrays} (\code{\verb~|Machine.release()~}, \code{\verb~|Machine.Spare~}, \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.spares=N~}, default 16, 0 = off): when a session is closed its machine is reset and its arrays (tables all zero, checked; slabs at most the kept size) go to a process-wide list, softly held; the next \code{\verb~|MachineSession~} starts with them instead of allocating and growing its own. A later pass of \code{\verb~|-X passes~}, a lane that is made again, the next file of a long-lived process pay the first growth once per process.}
\li{\strong{Per-worker retention} (\code{\verb~|Sizes.retainRecords / retainRunWords / retainTable / retainQuiet~}, \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.big=own~}): the first design, kept as a measured variant only: each machine keeps its slab at the size it grew to, up to 64 MiB, and gives it back after 1,024 quiet resets. Same garbage as (1), no copy at all in steady state, but the memory is held per worker (peak RSS +250 MiB on cedar and Std with 8 workers) and 16 closed sessions holding such slabs ran a 6 GB JVM of the driver's invariance suite out of memory. Not the default; INT6 may drop it (\code{\verb~|retained~}, \code{\verb~|decayPools~}, \code{\verb~|vhBig / rsBig~}).}
\li{\strong{Census} (always on, cold paths only): \code{\verb~|machine.alloc.grow|reset|pooled.<array>.2^k~}, \code{\verb~|machine.alloc.newBytes~}, \code{\verb~|machine.alloc.growCopyBytes~}, \code{\verb~|machine.session.records|runWords.2^k~}, \code{\verb~|machine.resetNanos~} / \code{\verb~|machine.growNanos~} (under statistics), \code{\verb~|machine.big.*~}, \code{\verb~|machine.adopted~}.}
\li{\strong{A hazard found on the way, fixed in the patch} (F-V1-M2): \code{\verb~|Machine.addPairAt~} wrote the pair entry and then grew the pair log; a growth that the heap refuses (\code{\verb~|HeapLimit~}) left an entry in the table that the log does not know, \code{\verb~|reset()~} clears logged entries only, and a grown table goes to the pool as "all zero": a later session could read a pair answer of a dead session under the same record ids. Needs a refused growth of a log of 32 MiB or more (8 M pairs in one session); never seen. The patch logs first.}
}

\p{Not changed: record and run formats, ids, hashes, ticks; the base sizes; the session cap.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Correctness}

\p{The prototype changes which \code{\verb~|int[]~} a session uses, never what is written into it. Evidence, all on the delivered tree (\code{\verb~|work/v1/tree5~}, jar 4e129e8cb0c7) unless a row says otherwise:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Check}\th{Result}}
\tr{\td{\strong{M8's gate} (\code{\verb~|report.sh gate --switches warm,gated,expect --session-mib 1~}, corpora tutorial, bugs, all 230 tests, Cedar slice, Init; private root \code{\verb~|work/v1/root~}; report \code{\verb~|work/v1/data/gate/machine-gate-v4.md~}; the same result on the shared-2 tree, \code{\verb~|machine-gate-v2.md~}, with the same tick totals)}\td{\strong{PASS WITH DECLINES: no wrong verdict, no difference.} Differential against the reference engine per declaration: SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, crash / timeout 0 (15,845 + 8,064 + 57,938 engine checks). Tier equivalence with the generic tier: 0 different outcomes. Convention equivalence (host depth 0 and unbounded against 128: outcome, ticks, call events, frame trace, every record word, run word and pair entry, whole sessions at every reset): 0 different. Audits (poison, scopeaudit, maskaudit; memocheck, shortcheck): 0 different, 0 ended by an audit. The real driver: 230 / 230 verdicts right, Cedar slice and Init right, 0 MACHINE-INCOMPLETE, 0 WARM-ANOMALY. The declines are the main tree's: eager reduction (1 + 4 + 11 + 164 units) and the three \code{\verb~|good/perf~} units whose 1 - 2 GiB slab does not fit the gate's 6 GB JVM (the same three as in INT6's gate of the main tree)}}
\tr{\td{\strong{Ticks, records, run words, sessions with ONE worker} (deterministic sessions; JVM, statistics) against the base jar}\td{cedar: 4,956 sessions, 137,704,790 ticks, 69,291,860 records, 400,583,117 run words; Std: 3,223 sessions, 106,442,803 ticks, 52,468,248 records, 266,604,091 run words: \strong{identical}, for the first prototype and for the final tree}}
\tr{\td{\strong{The driver's invariance} (1 / 2 / 4 / 8 workers, 1-byte session cap = a reset after every unit, pipelined with a publication after every 1 / 7 / 64 units; \code{\verb~|DriverBench --compare~} with the prototype jar as primary engine, \code{\verb~|work/v1/tools/invariance.sh~}: the suite's command with a private class path)}\td{published tests: \strong{231 files x 9 runs: 231 correct, 0 wrong, 0 declined, 0 schedule differences, 0 pipelined declines}. The big corpora (init-prelude, Init, Std, cedar; 8 GB JVMs): 4 files x 9 runs: 4 correct, 0 wrong, 0 schedule differences (on the shared-2 tree; the published tests were run again on every later tree: same result)}}
\tr{\td{Fast test group of the module (\code{\verb~|-Dgroups=fast~})}\td{412 tests, 0 failures, 3 skipped (first prototype and final tree)}}
}

\p{Two defects of EARLIER versions of the prototype, found by these checks and by the measurements, and what they changed in the design:}

\ul{
\li{\em{Per-worker retention with strongly held spares} (the first prototype): two of three 6 GB JVMs of the invariance suite died with \code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} (closed sessions' slabs of up to 64 MiB each, 16 sets), and the native image at \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~} ended pass 2 of mathlib with "Garbage-collected heap size exceeded". Hence the shared, softly held pool with a bound per size and the rule that a spare never carries a slab above the kept size.}
\li{\em{Giving a replaced slab to the shared pool at the growth}: code that holds a local copy of \code{\verb~|m.vh~} across a call that adds a record keeps reading the old array - harmless while the old array is garbage, wrong once another worker can write into it. Found by reading, before any run; hence the hand-over at the end of \code{\verb~|reset()~}.}
\li{\em{Holding the replaced steps of a slab strongly until the reset} (the tree before the delivered one, jar 1d6a18219223): M8's gate FAILED in its pipeline part with one \code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} (\code{\verb~|good/perf/magma-list-deep-n36~}, a declaration whose slab passes 512 MiB: the replaced 16 .. 256 MiB arrays doubled what it held; \code{\verb~|machine-gate-v3.md~}). Everything else of that gate was clean. Hence the soft hold and the rule that an array above the pooled size (64 MiB) is not kept; the delivered tree passes.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Measurements}

\p{Native image, 8 workers unless a row says otherwise, \code{\verb~|load=pipelined~}, \code{\verb~|passes-mode=reload~}; a row is one process with 4 - 5 passes (mathlib 3); the columns are the LATER passes (2..N): median wall, mean collections / pause time / bytes freed per pass, peak RSS of the process. Variants: \strong{base} = the base image; \strong{off} = the prototype's image with \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.big=off -Drlvm.machine.spares=0~} (the base behaviour, same code layout); \strong{shared-2} = the shared pool with 2 arrays per size and the exact next size (jar 68e9bdf1aeef): the tree all timing rows below were taken on; the \strong{delivered} tree (jar 4e129e8cb0c7) bounds the pool by bytes, lets a slab take a larger pooled array and holds replaced arrays softly - cold paths only; \strong{own} = the first prototype, per-worker retention; \strong{+ store / ref} = with the three proposals of section 7 in private copies (their first, unbounded version). Two values per cell = two processes in the same window, interleaved with the other variants.}

