\tr{\td{Std}\td{off}\td{1.87 / 1.84 s}\td{19 / 17}\td{118 / 111 ms}\td{6.3 / 6.0 \%}\td{701 / 664 MB}\td{700 / 699 MiB}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{\strong{shared-2}}\td{\strong{1.81 / 1.81 s}}\td{14 / 14}\td{90 / 84 ms}\td{5.0 / 4.6 \%}\td{522 / 512 MB}\td{822 / 788 MiB}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{own}\td{1.87 / 1.84 s}\td{16 / 14}\td{114 / 97 ms}\td{6.1 / 5.3 \%}\td{594 / 590 MB}\td{993 / 920 MiB}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{shared-2 + store / ref (first version)}\td{1.75 s}\td{11}\td{70 ms}\td{4.0 \%}\td{244 MB}\td{838 MiB}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{off, image of the \strong{delivered} tree (window of 21:17Z)}\td{2.35 s}\td{26}\td{159 ms}\td{6.8 \%}\td{1,070 MB}\td{868 MiB}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{\strong{delivered} (same window)}\td{\strong{2.26 s}}\td{18}\td{122 ms}\td{5.4 \%}\td{757 MB}\td{911 MiB}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{off, image of the delivered tree (same window)}\td{1.77 s}\td{18}\td{114 ms}\td{6.4 \%}\td{651 MB}\td{734 MiB}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{\strong{delivered} (same window)}\td{\strong{1.70 s}}\td{14}\td{87 ms}\td{5.1 \%}\td{521 MB}\td{800 MiB}}
}

\p{(The window of 21:17Z ran on a quieter host than the earlier ones: compare inside a window only. The delivered tree gives -3.8 \% on cedar and -4.0 \% on Std against its own image with the base behaviour, as shared-2 did: -5 \% and -2.4 \%.)}

\p{One worker (first prototype against base, 3 passes each, one window): cedar 15.49 -> 14.84 s, Std 11.29 -> 11.18 s; garbage 940 -> 678 MB and 588 -> 548 MB per pass, pauses 103 -> 86 ms and 70 -> 58 ms. The cedar difference is the drift of three passes (15.52, 15.11, 14.57), not a result: with one worker a collection stops one thread.}

\p{Reading: on the mid-size corpora the prototype takes a third of the garbage of a warm pass away (what the 8 sessions of every pass grew, and the slabs of the large sessions) and 3 - 5 \% of the wall clock; with the store and reference proposals 65 - 70 \% of the garbage and 6 - 8 \% of the wall clock. \strong{The pause share does not fall in proportion} (6.5 -> 5 -> 4.3 \%): what is left is the loader - every \code{\verb~|reload~} pass builds the store again (0.49 GB on cedar), and a young collection during the load copies what the loader has just made.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 mathlib (721,845 units; \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~}; windows of 19:48Z, 20:00Z, 20:31Z)}

\table{
\tr{\th{variant}\th{wall per pass}\th{CPU}\th{collections (complete ones)}\th{pauses}\th{pause share}\th{freed per pass}\th{peak RSS}}
\tr{\td{base}\td{31.39 / 31.52 / 31.42 s}\td{217.6 s}\td{365 (300)}\td{3.22 s}\td{10.2 \%}\td{20.70 GB}\td{3,510 MiB}}
\tr{\td{\strong{shared-2}}\td{\strong{29.87 / 29.67 / 30.22 s}}\td{210.3 s}\td{327 (300)}\td{2.89 s}\td{9.7 \%}\td{18.96 GB}\td{3,505 MiB}}
\tr{\td{\strong{delivered} (window of 21:18Z, no base row in that window)}\td{28.82 / 30.00 / 30.59 s}\td{212.5 s}\td{362}\td{3.09 s}\td{10.2 \%}\td{19.02 GB}\td{3,488 MiB}}
\tr{\td{own (first prototype)}\td{30.89 s, then \strong{OutOfMemoryError in pass 2}}\td{216.1 s}\td{313}\td{2.96 s}\td{9.6 \%}\td{19.41 GB}\td{3,826 MiB}}
\tr{\td{shared-2 + store / ref, FIRST version of the proposals (pools of up to 4 - 8 MiB per table, strongly held)}\td{\strong{OutOfMemoryError in pass 1}}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{3,883 MiB}}
\tr{\td{machine (bounded pool) + store / ref, BOUNDED version; one pass in a host slot on the loaded host: NOT a timing row (\code{\verb~|work/v1/data/stats/x3-mathlib-t8.out~})}\td{ACCEPT (70.5 s on the loaded host)}\td{}\td{\strong{249} (224)}\td{3.08 s}\td{}\td{\strong{12.07 GB}}\td{3,477 MiB}}
}

\p{The delivered tree on mathlib, machine census (native, \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~}, pipelined, one pass, statistics on, a host slot: NOT a timing run; \code{\verb~|work/v1/data/stats/census-v4*-mathlib-t8.*~}):}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{base behaviour}\th{delivered}}
\tr{\td{new machine arrays in the pass}\td{5,447 MB (records 2,609, runs 2,261, tables 578)}\td{\strong{3,407 MB} (records 1,720, runs 1,170, tables 517)}}
\tr{\td{freed by the collections}\td{20.85 GB}\td{18.70 GB}}
\tr{\td{collections / pauses (statistics on, loaded host)}\td{585 / 5.83 s}\td{546 / 4.79 s}}
\tr{\td{arrays taken from / given to the shared pool}\td{}\td{136 / 258; 5 expired; 0 bytes held at the end}}
}

\p{At 4 GB the pool cannot hold its arrays: 511 of the 546 collections are complete ones in a heap that is 2.8 GB live, and a complete collection under pressure takes softly held arrays - which is what "never the reason for an OutOfMemoryError" means. So on mathlib at \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~} the machine's garbage falls by 37 \%, not by 90 \%; in the 13 GB JVM (account above, shared-2) by 61 \%, on cedar by 98 \% from the second pass on (JFR, three reload passes, 8 workers: 1,847 MB -> 382 MB, of which 374 MB in the first pass).}

\p{Allocation account of one batch pass with the prototype and the (first) proposals (JVM, JFR, exact per thread; \code{\verb~|x2-mathlib-t8.alloc.txt~}): workers 13.83 -> \strong{4.45 GB}, process 17.4 -> 7.6 GB, 18 -> 7 collections in the JVM. By site: machine slabs 4.89 -> 1.76 GB and tables 0.56 -> 0.37 GB (shared-2; see below), \code{\verb~|IntIntMap~} 2.89 -> 0.21 GB, overlay index 2.37 -> 0.42 GB, rewriter memo 1.54 -> 0.02 GB; untouched: \code{\verb~|Overlay.grow~} 1.19 GB, level overlays 0.24 GB, the loader 3.11 GB.}

\p{Reading:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The prototype is worth 4.8 \% of the wall clock of a pipelined mathlib pass} (31.4 -> 29.9 s) and 3 \% of the CPU, for the same peak RSS. Most of that is not the pauses (3.22 -> 2.89 s) but the work around a fresh 8 - 64 MiB array: zeroing, page faults, the copy of the whole old slab instead of the words in use.}
\li{\strong{shared-2 misses too often}: the large sessions come in clusters (neighbouring units of the file are checked by all 8 workers at once), so more than two arrays of a size are wanted at the same time; of the run slabs to 8 MiB 19 were still allocated (123 before), to 16 MiB 19 (35), record slabs to 16 MiB 19 (71), to 32 MiB 10 (27), to 64 MiB 4 (4): 2.1 GB of the machine's 5.4 GB remained. Hence the delivered version: the pool is bounded by its bytes (a sixteenth of the heap, at most 512 MiB) instead of 2 per size, and a slab takes a pooled array of up to four times the size it asked for.}
\li{\strong{Per-worker retention is not an option}: it holds 8 times the largest declaration, saved less (the large sessions are spread over the workers), and ran out of the 4 GB heap in the second pass.}
\li{\strong{The pools proposed for the store and the reference kernel must be small.} Their first version (index tables to 4 MiB per overlay, map tables to 8 MiB per map, 9 maps per kernel, 3 lanes per worker) ran the image out of the 4 GB heap in the first pass. The patches in \code{\verb~|work/v1/patches/~} are the bounded version (1 MiB per overlay index, 512 KiB per map table: 2 MiB and 12 MiB per lane at most); with it and the machine patch a pipelined mathlib pass frees 12.1 GB instead of 20.7 GB in 249 collections instead of 365, at the base's peak RSS (last row of the table). That is where the garbage goes; of the 12 GB left, 3.1 GB are the loader's and about 3.4 GB the machine's slabs that a 4 GB heap does not let the pool keep.}
\li{\strong{The targets are not reached.} Pauses are 9.7 \% of the pass with the prototype (shared-2). At \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~} the heap is 2.6 GB live, 85 \% of the collections are complete ones and their number follows the garbage only weakly (365 -> 327 for 8 \% less garbage).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. The region question}

\p{\em{Is a worker's session better as ONE arena of ints that is reset by moving a pointer, with the tables sized once and cleared by epoch? What prevents it, and what would a two-region design save?}}

\p{\strong{It already is that arena, in two slabs.} Records (\code{\verb~|vh~}) and runs (\code{\verb~|rs~}) are filled by a bump pointer and a reset moves the two pointers back; the stacks (\code{\verb~|es~}, \code{\verb~|W~}, \code{\verb~|X~}, \code{\verb~|RS~}, \code{\verb~|SC~}) are LIFO; nothing is cleared in the slabs (a record and a run are written whole before they are read). What a reset does beyond two assignments is clear the tables in O(used): one fill of the 128 KiB / 64 KiB base tables (or one probe per record when the session was small), the pair entries through the log, an exchange of a grown table for the pooled base one. With the prototype no reset and no growth allocates in steady state. Section 2.3 has the price: a reset is 8 - 9 us, 0.2 - 0.3 \% of the worker CPU. A single \code{\verb~|int[]~} for both slabs, or tables cleared by an epoch per slot (which needs a word per slot or bits out of the 4-bit tag), cannot win more than that; \strong{after (2) there is no gap for a pointer-reset arena to close, so none was prototyped.} What costs more than the resets is table GROWTH: at the 1 MiB cap a session ends with 16,000 - 32,000 records (2,400 of 5,000 sessions on cedar), so nearly every session outgrows the 2^15-slot \code{\verb~|vtab~} once and re-probes its records (2,666 of 5,058 sessions on cedar took the pooled 2^16 table; \code{\verb~|rtab~} 794, pairs 730): 4 - 6 \% of the worker CPU is inside growths (section 2.3). "Sized once" in that sense is \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.sizes=14,16,16,14,12,10~} (a 256 KiB \code{\verb~|vtab~} from the start); that alone did not show in one row per corpus (section 2.3), so the right base sizes are a sweep, not a conclusion.}

\p{\strong{What prevents "everything a declaration made dies with it" is that warm sessions are worth having}, and the survivors are not a small, known set. One worker, exact counters (\code{\verb~|work/v1/tmp/batch-region.log~}):}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{cedar: ticks}\th{records}\th{sessions}\th{Std: ticks}\th{records}\th{sessions}}
\tr{\td{one declaration per session (\code{\verb~|gated,expect~}: mark / release per declaration)}\td{163.5 M}\td{88.6 M}\td{145,789}\td{127.7 M}\td{68.1 M}\td{100,907}}
\tr{\td{warm, cap 256 KiB}\td{153.9 M (-5.9 \%)}\td{79.9 M}\td{11,474}\td{120.8 M (-5.5 \%)}\td{61.7 M}\td{8,339}}
\tr{\td{warm, cap 1 MiB (the default)}\td{137.7 M (-15.8 \%)}\td{69.3 M (-21.8 \%)}\td{4,956}\td{106.4 M (-16.7 \%)}\td{52.5 M (-22.9 \%)}\td{3,223}}
\tr{\td{warm, cap 4 MiB}\td{121.0 M (-26.0 \%)}\td{59.0 M (-33.3 \%)}\td{2,839}\td{91.3 M (-28.5 \%)}\td{43.4 M (-36.3 \%)}\td{1,561}}
}

\p{At the default cap a declaration finds 22 \% of the records it needs already made by an earlier declaration of its session (133 of 613 per declaration on cedar) and saves 16 \% of its ticks. Which records: X1's census ([rep-X1](rep-X1), table C2, \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}): the tabling records of INFER / CHECK (39 - 58 \% found again), LAM closures (29 - 32 \%), application spines (19 - 20 \%), PI closures (16 - 19 \%); the tabling records of EVAL, the largest class, 7 - 18 \%. They refer to each other by record id, so a survivor drags its children along, and X1 found no cheap predictor at creation time.}

\p{\strong{What a two-region design could save is therefore not memory but ticks}: the reuse of a 4 MiB session (-26 \% ticks against -16 \%) at the table footprint of a 1 MiB one - F24 (NOTES-M6) measured that the 4 MiB session is SLOWER in CPU time although it needs 12 \% fewer ticks, because its tables leave the cache. A survivor region would have to be filled by evacuation at the reset (copy the records that a LATER declaration found, with their children, to the front; rebuild the tables; remap or drop every memo word that holds an id): a copying collector for the machine's own heap, with a bit per record set on a cross-declaration hit. Upper bound of the gain: the 10 \% of ticks between the 1 MiB and the 4 MiB row, less the evacuation; in JVM garbage it saves nothing. Not prototyped; it is an experiment for the value-store owners (M1 / X1's line of work), not a memory-management fix.}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Findings for other owners}

\table{
\tr{\th{To}\th{Finding}\th{Evidence}\th{Proposal}}
\tr{\td{LP2 (\code{\verb~|rlvm-store~}, \code{\verb~|Overlay~})}\td{\strong{F-V1-S1}: the overlay's hash-consing index is reallocated at every shrink and every doubling. \code{\verb~|resetTo~} cuts it back to \code{\verb~|KEEP_TABLE~} through \code{\verb~|rehash~} (a new 128 KiB array), \code{\verb~|insert~} doubles it again (a new array per step); the reference kernel calls \code{\verb~|resetTo~} once per declaration}\td{mathlib pass, 8 workers: 4,354 + 3,567 tables, 2.37 GB = 17 \% of the workers' bytes; cedar 131 MB of 1,165 MB}\td{\code{\verb~|patches/rlvm-store-LP2/0001-overlay-index-pool.patch~} (85 lines): retired tables pooled by size per overlay, all zero, up to 4 MiB. Measured in a private copy (cedar, JFR, one cold pass): 131 -> 53 MB (the first table of each size). The pool is not in \code{\verb~|usedBytes()~}; a decay like \code{\verb~|Machine.Big~} is the owner's choice}}
\tr{\td{LP2 (\code{\verb~|rlvm-store~}, \code{\verb~|TermOps.Memo~})}\td{\strong{F-V1-S2}: \code{\verb~|widen()~} allocates two temporaries (\code{\verb~|long[size]~}, \code{\verb~|int[size]~}) at every doubling, and every rewriter call starts again at 64 slots}\td{mathlib: 1.54 GB = 11 \%; cedar 106 MB}\td{\code{\verb~|0002-rewriter-memo-scratch.patch~} (42 lines): the scratch is kept with the tables. cedar: 106 -> 17 MB}}
\tr{\td{LP2 (\code{\verb~|rlvm-store~}, \code{\verb~|Overlay.grow~}, lanes)}\td{\strong{F-V1-S3} (no patch): the node array is kept up to 32 MiB per overlay, so its growth is (a) the first growth of every lane: 3 lanes per worker, new for every pass of \code{\verb~|-X passes~} (cedar: 235 MB of 1,165 MB in a cold pass), (b) one declaration of the reference kernel that needs 256 MiB (0.9 GB of copies on mathlib), (c) \code{\verb~|releaseNodes~} (a new 32 MiB array per shrink)}\td{mathlib 1.40 GB = 10 \%}\td{lanes (worker contexts) that survive a pass, or a spare list like \code{\verb~|Machine.Spare~}; for (b) growth by 1.5 or a shared large-array pool}}
\tr{\td{reference owners (\code{\verb~|rlvm-ref~}, \code{\verb~|IntIntMap~})}\td{\strong{F-V1-R1}: every cache of the kernel is cut back to 256 entries at \code{\verb~|clear()~} when it is large or sparse and doubles again in the next large declaration; table and slot list are new at every step}\td{mathlib: 2.89 GB = 21 \% (100,000 arrays); cedar 186 MB}\td{\code{\verb~|patches/rlvm-ref/0001-intintmap-table-pool.patch~} (101 lines): the tables of a map go round (retired ones zeroed through the slot list, O(entries)), up to 8 MiB. cedar: 186 -> 50 MB. Pooled tables are not in \code{\verb~|bytes()~}}}
\tr{\td{E1, INT6}\td{the four rows above are the reference kernel re-checking what the machine declines: 61 \% of the workers' bytes on mathlib come from 0.93 \% of the units}\td{section 2.1}\td{keeping the eager-reduction units on the machine removes the source}}
\tr{\td{M6 / INT6 (\code{\verb~|Machine.addPairAt~})}\td{\strong{F-V1-M2}: a refused growth of the pair log leaves a pair entry that no reset clears (section 3, item 5)}\td{by reading; needs a 32 MiB log and a full heap}\td{in the patch: log first}}
\tr{\td{M6 / INT6 (\code{\verb~|Machine.Sizes~})}\td{\strong{F-V1-M3}: at the 1 MiB cap most sessions outgrow their base tables once; 4.4 - 5.6 \% of the worker CPU is inside growths (re-probes, copies), 0.2 - 0.3 \% inside resets}\td{section 2.3 (\code{\verb~|machine.growNanos~}, \code{\verb~|machine.resetNanos~})}\td{a sweep of the base table sizes at the 1 MiB cap; a 2^16 \code{\verb~|vtab~} alone did not show}}
\tr{\td{LP2, level store (P7-F5)}\td{new lanes build level overlays of 1 - 2 MiB each: 0.24 GB per mathlib pass in batch mode; nothing new beyond P7-F5}\td{section 2.1}\td{}}
\tr{\td{P7 (driver), steward}\td{\code{\verb~|-X stats-file~} itself allocates: \code{\verb~|RunStats.currentThreadSched~} was 137 MB of a cedar run (7 \% of the workers' bytes, JFR with default TLABs): the time account is not free in garbage}\td{\code{\verb~|work/v1/data/jfr~} first run}\td{read \code{\verb~|/proc/thread-self/schedstat~} into a reused buffer}}
\tr{\td{steward (launcher)}\td{with \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~} 85 \% of the collections of a mathlib pass are complete collections (the heap is 2.6 GB live of 4 GB): the pause share at 4 GB is the heap setting as much as the garbage}\td{section 5}\td{measure the image with \code{\verb~|-Xmx6g~} before judging the collector}}
}

\p{The three proposals (bounded version) in the private copies: unit tests of \code{\verb~|rlvm-store~} 71 run, 0 failures, 1 skipped; of \code{\verb~|rlvm-ref~} 136 run, 0 failures, 7 skipped (\code{\verb~|$MVN -o -q -f work/v1/xtree/<module>/pom.xml test~}). Measured effect of the FIRST version, one cold pass over cedar with 8 workers (JFR): workers 1,165 MB (base) -> 1,023 MB (machine prototype) -> 879 MB (with the proposals); over mathlib (13 GB JVM): 13.83 -> 4.45 GB.}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Not established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The two targets of the brief.} Collections under 2 \% of a pipelined 8-worker pass on mathlib, and one process within 2 \% of the 8-shard ceiling: not reached with the machine alone (section 5), and the ceiling itself was not measured again (P7: 26.6 - 26.8 s for a check-only pass; the quiet lock was 10 - 15 minutes per window, a ceiling run is eight mathlib loads at once). What keeps the pause share where it is after the workers' garbage is gone is the loader in \code{\verb~|reload~} passes (3.1 GB per load, and every young collection copies what it has just built) and, at \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~}, a heap that is 2.6 GB live: neither is the machine's.}
\li{\strong{JVM launcher timings}: none. All timing rows are the native image; the JVM was used for the allocation account, the census, the gate and the invariance suite only.}
\li{\strong{One worker with the final prototype}: only the first prototype has one-worker rows (cedar, Std: inside the spread of three passes). A one-worker process has no shared collector to wait for; nothing is expected.}
\li{\strong{mathlib with the image's default heap} (13 GB): every mathlib row is \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~}, where 85 \% of the collections are complete ones; the pause times do not transfer to another heap setting.}
\li{\strong{The store and reference proposals} were measured in private copies (store: the published jar f85c730603b8 with the \code{\verb~|Overlay~} / \code{\verb~|TermOps~} classes of the copy, because LP2's working tree had unpublished changes in \code{\verb~|ProofBlocks~}). They changed no verdict in the runs here (cedar, Std, mathlib: ACCEPT; the unit tests of the two copies: section 7), but their owners' gates have not seen them.}
\li{\strong{Timing with the bounded store / reference pools}: the window (cedar, Std, mathlib) waited behind the measurement lock for more than ten minutes (the lock is not fair; waits of 15 - 30 minutes per window in the last hour) and was withdrawn when this report was closed. What is established for the bounded version: it passes mathlib at \code{\verb~|-Xmx4g~} with the base's peak RSS, with 249 collections and 12.1 GB freed where the base has 365 and 20.7 GB (one pass on the loaded host); its wall clock is not measured. The timed \code{\verb~|+ store / ref~} rows of cedar and Std are the first, unbounded version.}
\li{\strong{mathlib, delivered tree against base in ONE window}: the delivered row (30.3 s) and the base row (31.4 s) are 90 minutes apart; the same-window pair is shared-2 against base 40 minutes apart (29.9 / 31.4 s). The -4.8 \% is therefore weaker evidence than the cedar and Std pairs; the CPU (217.6 -> 210 - 212 s) and the garbage (20.7 -> 19.0 GB) agree in all three prototype rows.}
\li{\strong{Decay and bounds of the shared pool} (\code{\verb~|big.decay~} 8,192 resets, \code{\verb~|big.mib~}) are first guesses, not swept.}
\li{\strong{The first growth of the other modules' lanes} in every pass (store overlay, level overlays, reference kernel tables): seen in the account, not addressed.}
\li{\strong{A unit test for F-V1-M2} (the pair entry left by a refused growth of the log): none; the heap cannot be exhausted cheaply in a test.}
}
}
